target.body:
Closes #106569.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where users could lose OpenAI Responses message boundaries when two separate assistant message items are emitted in the same phase and the second message starts with the first message's text, such as `Hello` followed by `Hello world!`.

The previous generic collapse rule treated that pattern as a cumulative snapshot and replaced the first message block, corrupting transcript structure and stream lifecycle events.

## Why This Change Was Made

The generic snapshot collapse rule now requires the prior and next message to share the same normalized Responses item id or the same Responses `output_index`, plus the same phase, before replacing the prior text block. Distinct `output_index` values are treated as distinct output items, even when their text is prefix-related.

The already-shipped Bedrock Mantle compatibility path remains broader because the raw provider evidence in #91959 reports rotating message items and corrupted non-streaming `output` arrays. For `amazon-bedrock-mantle` routes, strict-prefix same-phase adjacent snapshots still collapse across item ids so affected users do not regress to repeated cumulative replies.

## User Impact

OpenAI Responses chats preserve separate assistant message blocks on ordinary indexed provider streams. Bedrock Mantle Responses streams that emit cumulative rotating-id snapshots still render as one updated text block instead of duplicate repeated text.

No configuration, migration, or operator action is required.

## Evidence

- `git diff --check` passed.
- `node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p test/tsconfig/tsconfig.core.test.agents.json --incremental --tsBuildInfoFile .artifacts/tsgo-cache/core-test-agents.tsbuildinfo` passed.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs packages/ai/src/providers/openai-responses-shared.test.ts` passed: 1 file, 71 tests.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/openai-transport-stream.base.test.ts` passed: 1 file, 45 tests.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/openai-transport-stream.streaming.test.ts` passed: 1 file, 43 tests.
- Dependency contract checked locally: `node_modules/openai/resources/responses/responses.d.ts` declares `output_index: number` on both `ResponseOutputItemAddedEvent` and `ResponseOutputItemDoneEvent`.
- #91959 includes raw-provider evidence that Bedrock Mantle can emit many cumulative message items in both streaming and non-streaming Responses output; this PR keeps cross-item strict-prefix collapse only for that provider route.
- Attempted `node scripts/check-changed.mjs -- packages/ai/src/providers/openai-responses-stream-compat.ts packages/ai/src/providers/openai-responses-shared.ts src/agents/openai-responses-transport.ts packages/ai/src/providers/openai-responses-shared.test.ts src/agents/openai-transport-stream.base.test.ts`; the delegated validation backend failed sanity checks before project checks ran.

## Real behavior proof

**Behavior addressed:** Separate same-phase Responses message items keep distinct text blocks on ordinary OpenAI Responses routes, while Bedrock Mantle cumulative rotating-id snapshots still collapse in both stream processors.

**Environment tested:** Local Linux checkout, Node v22.22.0, existing dependency install.

**Steps run after the patch:** Invoked the actual patched shared `processResponsesStream` function from `packages/ai/src/providers/openai-responses-shared.ts` and the actual patched agent transport `processResponsesStream` function exposed through the agent transport test API, using representative Responses stream events for both distinct-prefix messages and Bedrock Mantle same-phase rotating-id cumulative snapshots.

**Evidence after fix:** Terminal output from the production function calls:

```text
{
  "sharedDistinctPrefix": {
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hello",
        "textSignature": "{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_1\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hello world!",
        "textSignature": "{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_2\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"
      }
    ],
    "events": [
      "text_start:0",
      "text_end:0",
      "text_start:1",
      "text_end:1"
    ]
  },
  "sharedBedrockCumulative": {
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hello world!",
        "textSignature": "{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_snap_2\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"
      }
    ],
    "events": [
      "text_start:0",
      "text_end:0",
      "text_end:0"
    ]
  },
  "agentDistinctPrefix": {
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hello",
        "textSignature": "{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_1\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hello world!",
        "textSignature": "{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_2\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"
      }
    ],
    "events": [
      "text_start:0",
      "text_end:0",
      "text_start:1",
      "text_end:1"
    ]
  },
  "agentBedrockCumulative": {
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hello world!",
        "textSignature": "{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_snap_2\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"
      }
    ],
    "events": [
      "text_start:0",
      "text_end:0",
      "text_end:0"
    ]
  }
}
```

**Observed result:** The distinct-prefix cases produced two text blocks and separate start/end stream events. The Bedrock Mantle cumulative cases used rotating ids with no `output_index` and produced one final text block while keeping the cumulative update behavior on index 0 in both processors.

**Not tested:** A live OpenAI or Bedrock Mantle network stream with real credentials was not exercised; the Bedrock Mantle compatibility proof uses the provider route and event shape described in #91959's raw-provider evidence.
